      </div>
      <h1>Checkout Percentage Explained</h1>
      <p class="lead-text">Trebles for show, doubles for dough. Checkout percentage measures how efficiently a player converts finishing opportunities into won legs — here is how it is calculated, what counts as a good percentage, and why clinical finishing decides big matches.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container content-layout">
    <main class="main-column">
      <VerificationBadge verifiedDate="October 2026" topic="Checkout Percentage in Darts" />

      <section class="content-card">
<p>In professional and grassroots darts, television broadcasts frequently highlight the phrase: <strong>"Trebles for show, doubles for dough."</strong></p>
<p>While explosive maximums and 180s captivate spectators at Alexandra Palace, matches are ultimately decided on the outer ring of the board. A player can produce overwhelming power scoring with a 105 three-dart average, but if they miss their required finishing doubles, an opponent with a lower scoring average can step up and win the leg.</p>
<p>The metric that evaluates this finishing efficiency is <strong>checkout percentage</strong> (commonly labeled on broadcast graphics as <strong>doubles percentage</strong> or <strong>checkout efficiency</strong>).</p>
<p>This guide provides a comprehensive, source-grounded breakdown of how checkout percentage is calculated, how scoring platforms track attempts at a double alongside official <a href="https://www.thedra.co.uk/dra-rulebook">Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) rules</a> for finishing and busts, realistic benchmarks from grassroots pub leagues to world champion standards, and why finishing percentage plays such a decisive role in match outcomes.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Definition and formula</h2>
<p>Checkout percentage measures a player's accuracy in converting finishing opportunities into won legs.</p>
<h3>The standard explanatory convention</h3>
<p>In darts broadcasting and tutorial analysis, checkout percentage is commonly explained using an individual-dart conversion convention:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  <div class="formula-math">
    <span class="formula-label">Checkout Percentage</span> =
    <span class="fraction">
      <span class="numerator">Checkouts Completed (Winning Doubles Landed)</span>
      <span class="denominator">Total Individual Darts Aimed at a Finishing Double</span>
    </span>
    &times; 100
  </div>
</div>
<p>For instance, if a player throws at a finishing double 10 times across a match and successfully lands the required double 4 times to win 4 legs, their conversion rate is:</p>
<p class="formula-line"><code>4 &divide; 10 &times; 100 = 40.0%</code></p>
<div class="note-box">
<p><strong>Explanatory convention vs proprietary definitions:</strong> The formula above serves as an intuitive explanatory convention to interpret broadcast graphics such as 4/10 (40.0%). It is <strong>not</strong> a universal statutory definition codified by the Darts Regulation Authority (DRA), nor is it confirmed to be applied identically across every broadcaster, digital scoring platform (such as <a href="https://app.dartconnect.com/">DartConnect</a>), or sports database. While the DRA regulates official playing laws (including legal double-out finishes and bust score restoration), statistical tracking protocols, denominator definitions, and bust-handling rules are determined independently by individual data providers and scoring platforms.</p>
</div>
<h3>Individual double-attempt success rate vs match-level checkout percentage</h3>
<p>The phrase "checkout percentage" is applied to distinct metrics across broadcast analysis and academic research:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Individual Double-Attempt Success Rate (Broadcast Doubles Percentage):</strong> the practical explanatory convention is successful finishing-double darts divided by total individual darts aimed at a legal finishing double, &times; 100. This is the primary metric displayed on television broadcast graphics during a match (e.g. "Doubles: 4/10 — 40.0%"). In professional tour analytics, it is evaluated across specified rolling windows — such as Christopher Kempf's report of Jonny Clayton leading Tour Card holders with 43% double accuracy over his <strong>latest 200 legs played</strong> (<a href="https://www.pdc.tv/news/2026/october/14/Form-Guide--Clayton-the-checkout-king/">PDC Form Guide, 14 Oct 2026</a>). Clayton's individual 200-leg statistic does not establish a tour-wide norm or season-long average.</li>
<li><strong>Study-Defined Match-Level Checkout Percentage (Archival Research):</strong> in peer-reviewed sports science, Greve, van Meurs &amp; Strauss (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10390587/"><em>Scientific Reports</em> / PMC10390587</a>) explicitly define checkout percentage (CP) as a player's percentage of successful double-field hits to finish a leg, analysed at the individual match-performance level across N = 26,724 individual match performances from 442 players (2018–2021 via Darts Orakel). This metric captures match-performance-level finishing efficiency in econometric models, rather than serving as a universal broadcast denominator counting every individual dart thrown.</li>
</ol>
<h3>What counts as an "attempt at a double"?</h3>
<p>The <a href="https://www.thedra.co.uk/dra-rulebook">Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) Rulebook</a> governs the official rules of play (such as Rule 5.2.3 defining the 50-point inner bullseye and Rule 5.4.1 defining legal double-out finishes and bust restorations). The DRA rulebook does not mandate statistical formulas or define how broadcasters and software providers record attempts.</p>
<p>In standard broadcast and scoring platform practice, double attempts are logged according to the following conventions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Direct shots at a finishing double:</strong> when a player's remaining score is an even number between 2 and 40 (or an outshot where a double is mathematically required), any dart thrown directly at that finishing double is logged as an attempt. A hit that concludes the leg counts as 1 attempt and 1 hit in the numerator; a miss outside or into the adjacent single bed counts as 1 attempt and 0 hits.</li>
<li><strong>The 50-point bullseye counts as a double:</strong> under <strong>DRA Rule 5.2.3 and Rule 5.4.1</strong>, the central red Bullseye (inner bull) is worth 50 points and legally functions as a double (double 25). Therefore, any dart aimed at the Bullseye to conclude an outshot (such as on 170, 164, 124, or 50) is counted as an attempt at a double under standard broadcast conventions.</li>
<li><strong>Setup darts do NOT count:</strong> a dart thrown at a single or treble to reduce a score down to a preferred double is a <strong>setup shot</strong>, not a checkout attempt. For example, if a player has 80 points remaining and throws at Single 16 to leave 64, or Single 20 from 60 to leave 40, those darts are setup throws. Only the final dart thrown at the double counts toward the checkout percentage denominator.</li>
<li><strong>Scoring rules for bust visits:</strong> under <strong>DRA Rule 5.4.1 and Rule 5.4.2 (The Bust Rule)</strong>, if a player scores more points than required, leaves a remaining score of 1, or reduces their score to 0 without landing on a legal double, a bust occurs. When a bust occurs:
<ul>
<li><strong>Score restoration:</strong> the player's score <strong>strictly reverts back to the exact score they held at the beginning of that visit/turn</strong> (prior to throwing their first dart of that turn).</li>
<li><strong>Statistical denominator nuance:</strong> official playing rules govern score restoration, but do not dictate proprietary software algorithms or data provider rules. For example, if a player starts a turn on 32 points, hits Single 16 (leaving 16), and then accidentally lands in Double 16, they bust their visit, and under DRA Rule 5.4.1 their score strictly reverts to 32 (not 16). In statistical tracking, whether that second errant dart is recorded in the denominator as a double attempt depends on the specific scoring platform, tracking prompt, or scorekeeper convention. No universal cross-platform tracking standard has been verified across all digital scoring applications.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Darts that hit a double ring without finishing the leg:</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Errant darts causing a bust:</strong> if a player requires 16 points and accidentally lands in Double 16, they score 32 points, causing a bust under DRA Rule 5.4.1. The turn is voided, and the score reverts to the start of the visit. Because the dart did not successfully win the leg, it never counts as a successful checkout in the numerator (0 hits). In statistical tracking, whether an errant bust dart is recorded as a double attempt depends on the specific scoring software or scorekeeper convention; it never counts as a successful checkout in the numerator.</li>
<li><strong>Accidental doubles on setup visits:</strong> if a player is on a score where no double can finish the leg (e.g. 100 points) and an errant throw intended for Single 20 hits Double 20, that throw simply scores 40 points toward score reduction. Because it was not a finishing shot, it does not count in either the numerator or the denominator of checkout percentage.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>To explore valid outshots and checkout paths, consult our <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">Complete Darts Checkout Chart</a> or test combinations with our <a href="/tools/checkout-calculator/">Interactive Checkout Calculator</a>.</p>
      </section>

