</table></div>
<h3>Breakdown of the calculation</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Total legs contested:</strong> 5</li>
<li><strong>Total legs won (checkouts hit):</strong> 4</li>
<li><strong>Total darts thrown at a finishing double:</strong> 9</li>
<li><strong>Overall match checkout percentage:</strong> <strong>44.4%</strong> (4 &divide; 9 = 0.4444...)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Arithmetic and rounding check</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>After Leg 1:</strong> 1 &divide; 2 = <strong>50.0%</strong></li>
<li><strong>After Leg 2:</strong> 2 &divide; 4 = <strong>50.0%</strong></li>
<li><strong>After Leg 3, Visit 1:</strong> 2 &divide; 5 = <strong>40.0%</strong></li>
<li><strong>After Leg 3, Visit 2:</strong> 2 &divide; 7 = <strong>28.57%</strong> (rounds to <strong>28.6%</strong>)</li>
<li><strong>After Leg 4:</strong> 3 &divide; 8 = <strong>37.5%</strong></li>
<li><strong>After Leg 5 (Final):</strong> 4 &divide; 9 = <strong>44.44%</strong> (rounds to <strong>44.4%</strong>)</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice the mathematical swing in <strong>Leg 3</strong>: missing three double opportunities across two visits dragged the player's cumulative rate down from 50.0% to 28.6%. Clinical single-dart finishes on 170 and 32 in the following legs restored the overall conversion rate to 44.4%.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card">
<h2>What is a good checkout percentage?</h2>
<p>What constitutes a "good" checkout percentage depends heavily on sample size and competitive environment. In short single matches (e.g. best of 9 legs), individual percentages are volatile because a player may only throw 5 to 10 total darts at a double. Over an entire tournament or season, however, reliable statistical patterns emerge.</p>
<h3>Performance benchmarks</h3>
<p>The table below outlines observed professional benchmarks alongside indicative community rules of thumb:</p>
<div class="table-responsive"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">Tier</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Checkout Percentage Range</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Context &amp; Level</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Empirical Source &amp; Evidence Details</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>World-Class Single-Match Peak</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>50.0% – 60.0%+</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Elite major matchplay (unplayable form)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><em>Illustrative short-sample peak:</em> Observed in televised PDC match centers in short matches or weekend tournament runs; not an audited seasonal standard.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Top Professional / Major Contender</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>40.0% – 45.0%</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Indicative elite performance range</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><em>Illustrative benchmark only:</em> Published PDC Form Guide analysis by Christopher Kempf (<a href="https://www.pdc.tv/news/2026/october/14/Form-Guide--Clayton-the-checkout-king/">Form Guide: Clayton the checkout king</a>, 14 Oct 2026) reports Jonny Clayton leading Tour Card holders with 43% double accuracy over his latest 200 legs played. Clayton's individual rolling-window statistic does not establish a tour-wide average or prove a universal 40%–45% Top 16 standard.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>PDC Tour Card Holder Baseline</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>35.0% – 40.0%</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Approximate tour-level standard</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><em>Unverified editorial approximation:</em> Frequently cited in commentary as an informal professional baseline, but not substantiated by a published population-wide census table or verified multi-tournament dataset.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Competitive Super League / County</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>25.0% – 33.0%</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Advanced amateur and county leagues</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><em>Illustrative estimate:</em> Informal editorial guideline only. No centralized dataset, published scoreboards, or empirical studies exist for this tier.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Pub League / Local Club</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>18.0% – 25.0%</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Weekly recreational pub divisions</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><em>Illustrative estimate:</em> Informal editorial guideline only. Represents an illustrative range without verified empirical data.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Beginner / Casual Home Player</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>10.0% – 18.0%</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Novice home and social players</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><em>Illustrative estimate:</em> Informal editorial guideline only. Characterized by frequent visits to close legs; no empirical study supports this range.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><em>Note on sources: The professional figures above are illustrative only. Jonny Clayton's 43% figure is specifically a PDC-reported rolling analyst statistic over his latest 200 legs played, and does not demonstrate a universal 40%–45% elite standard or a tour-wide average. The 35%–40% baseline is an unverified editorial approximation without an accessible population-wide census table. World-class match peaks, county, pub, and beginner figures are purely informal illustrative estimates; no centralized league scoreboards or representative empirical datasets exist for grassroots darts.</em></p>
<h3>The mathematical meaning of the 40% benchmark</h3>
<p>Commentators frequently cite <strong>40%</strong> as the benchmark of a winning performance on television.</p>
<p>Mathematically, a 40% conversion rate corresponds to an average of <strong>2.5 individual double attempts per successful conversion over a sufficiently large sample</strong>:</p>
<p class="formula-line"><code>1 &divide; 0.40 = 2.5 individual double attempts per checkout hit</code></p>
<p>Over an extended match or tournament, this means an elite finisher requires an average of 25 double attempts to secure 10 winning legs.</p>
<p>However, this ratio does <strong>not</strong> guarantee a specific leg length or scoring average on its own. A player's overall three-dart average and the number of darts required to win a leg depend on their scoring cadence in non-finishing visits, the number of setup throws required, whether missed doubles resulted in lost legs, and how quickly their opponent applied pressure. Checkout percentage measures finishing conversion in isolation; it does not dictate a guaranteed 12-to-15-dart leg or a 100-point match average.</p>
      </section>

      <AdSlot position="in-article" />

      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Checkout percentage vs 3-dart average: which matters more?</h2>
<p>The debate over whether power scoring (<a href="/darts/average-explained/">3-Dart Average</a>) or clinical finishing (<a href="/players/checkout-percentage-explained/">Checkout Percentage</a>) is more decisive is a central topic in modern darts analytics.</p>
<p>Rather than viewing them in opposition, sports data researchers and PDC analysts recognize that both metrics interact to determine match outcomes:</p>
<div class="table-responsive"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">Performance Metric</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Player A (Hypothetical Power Scorer)</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Player B (Hypothetical Clinical Finisher)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3-Dart Average</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>104.20</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>93.80</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Doubles Converted</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">2 of 12</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>6 of 9</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Checkout Percentage</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">16.7% (Severe Double Trouble)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>66.7% (Devastating Efficiency)</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Hypothetical Match Outcome</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Lost 2–6</td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Won 6–2</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class="note-box">
<p><strong>Illustrative context:</strong> In this hypothetical scenario, Player A outscored Player B by more than 10 points per visit. However, by squandering 10 darts at doubles across multiple visits, Player A granted Player B the extra visits needed to break throw and win the match.</p>
</div>
<h3>The "false high average" phenomenon</h3>
<p>Peer-reviewed sports analytics research explores the interaction and distinct characteristics of scoring averages and checkout conversion:</p>
<ul>
<li>In an archival study of N = 26,724 professional match performances from 442 players (2018–2021), Greve, van Meurs &amp; Strauss (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10390587/"><em>Scientific Reports</em> / PMC10390587</a>) examined the relationship between match-level checkout percentage and three-dart average, observing a moderate correlation (r = .41).</li>
<li>The authors discuss CP and 3DA as complementary but separate performance indicators: checkout percentage offers higher content validity for finishing efficiency, whereas its statistical reliability is constrained by the smaller number of attempts per leg compared to general scoring visits.</li>
<li>Traditional three-dart averages can also reflect leg dynamics: missed darts at doubles record zero points, which mechanically lowers that leg's visit average, whereas a player whose opponent checks out rapidly may have their average calculated purely from uninterrupted high-scoring setup visits.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Situational pressure, timing, and environmental factors</h3>
<p>A raw checkout percentage does not capture the situational context or timing of when a double is attempted:</p>
<ul>
<li>In a study analysing 32,274 checkout-related observations from 122 professional players across PDC tournaments (April 2017 to September 2018), Ötting et al. (<a href="https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0230528"><em>PLOS ONE</em>, 2020</a>) investigated performance under within-leg pressure (operationalized by players' and opponents' chances of checking out). The authors found no evidence of a systematic overall choking or excelling effect under their defined pressure conditions, indicating robust motor stability among tour professionals.</li>
<li>External social and environmental factors can also interact with fine motor coordination. Greve et al. (2023) evaluated audience conditions across 26,724 professional match performances and found small negative associations between audience presence and checkout percentage in their multilevel models. While the reported real-audience and simulated-audience effects were statistically significant, the authors characterized the effect sizes as small, emphasizing substantial within-player variability.</li>
<li>In matchplay, clinical finishers distinguish themselves by high conversion on <strong>"first-dart doubles"</strong> when an opponent is sitting on an outshot, eliminating the opponent's opportunity to return to the oche.</li>
</ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Where to see checkout percentage stats</h2>
<p>To follow verified checkout statistics across professional circuits and check your own finishing progress, consult the following resources:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>PDC tournament coverage:</strong> Matches broadcast on television feature match-level checkout percentages on graphic overlays. For curated player analysis, visit our <a href="/players/stats/">PDC Player Stats Hub</a>.</li>
<li><strong>PDC all-time records:</strong> Historic scoring achievements and notable match checkout performances. Explore our <a href="/players/records/">PDC All-Time Records Directory</a>.</li>
<li><strong>World Darts Championship records:</strong> Tournament records and milestones from Alexandra Palace. See our <a href="/players/world-championship-records/">World Championship Records Dossier</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Digital scoring platforms (e.g. DartConnect):</strong> Widely utilized across competitive floor events and grassroots leagues for match scoring; specific double tracking and summary stats depend on application settings and league configurations.</li>
<li><strong>Finishing math resources:</strong> Review optimal paths from 170 to 2 in our <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">PDC Checkout Chart</a> or test checkouts with our <a href="/tools/checkout-calculator/">Interactive Checkout Calculator</a>.</li>
</ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is considered a good checkout percentage in darts?</h3>
            <p>In professional darts, an individual checkout percentage between <strong>40% and 45%</strong> is widely regarded as an illustrative elite benchmark, highlighted by PDC stats analyst Christopher Kempf's report of Jonny Clayton leading Tour Card holders with <strong>43% double accuracy over his latest 200 legs</strong> (PDC Form Guide, October 2026) — an individual rolling-window analyst statistic, not a tour-wide norm or season-long average. Across the broader professional circuit, <strong>35% to 40%</strong> is an unverified editorial approximation frequently mentioned in commentary, rather than an audited population-wide census. At the amateur level, where no centralized database or published scoreboard dataset exists, all figures are purely illustrative estimates: competitive county standards are informally estimated at roughly <strong>25% to 33%</strong>, local pub leagues at <strong>18% to 25%</strong>, and beginners learning double-out math at <strong>10% to 18%</strong>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Does a dart thrown at Bullseye count as a double attempt?</h3>
            <p><strong>Yes, in legal rules of play, but recording depends on provider conventions.</strong> Under Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) rules (Sections 5.2.3 and 5.4.1), the central 50-point red Bullseye (inner bull) is legally defined as a double (an inner ring worth twice the 25-point outer bull) and satisfies the requirement to finish on a double. Any dart thrown at the 50-point Bullseye when an outshot is mathematically achievable (such as 170, 164, 124, or 50) is treated as a double attempt under standard broadcast conventions. However, the DRA rulebook governs rules of play, not statistical tracking: whether and how a provider labels the dart in a statistical feed depends on that provider's tracking convention.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is the difference between checkout percentage and 3-dart average?</h3>
            <p>A <strong>3-dart average</strong> measures the average points scored across all three-dart visits, reflecting overall scoring power. A <strong>checkout percentage</strong> measures finishing efficiency by dividing successful checkouts by the total individual darts aimed at a double. While scoring average determines how quickly a player arrives at an outshot, checkout percentage determines whether they convert that opportunity into a won leg.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>How does missing a setup dart affect checkout percentage?</h3>
            <p>A dart thrown at a single or treble to reduce a score down to a double (such as throwing at Single 20 from 60 to leave Double 20) is a <strong>setup shot</strong>, not an attempt at a double. Setup shots do <strong>not</strong> count toward the checkout percentage denominator. Only darts thrown directly at a finishing double or the 50-point Bullseye when an outshot is mathematically achievable are logged as double attempts under standard conventions.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What happens to a player's score during a bust?</h3>
            <p>Under <strong>DRA Rule 5.4.1</strong>, if a player scores more points than required, leaves a remaining score of 1, or reduces their score to 0 without hitting a valid double, a bust occurs. The player's turn ends immediately, and their score <strong>reverts back to the exact score they held at the start of that visit</strong> (before throwing the first dart of that turn).</p>
          </div>
      </section>

